e berlanti world i  i loved working there and you know if  they ever need me back i'm happy to go  shoot arrows again  acting didn't start out as a planned  career for you but you went  from stage to film and tv you've been  you know working  pretty consistently when you realized  that this was what you actually wanted  to do  what did you envision for yourself  wouldn't being a successful actor look  like in the beginning  it's funny you ask that because that's  changed over the years and that's  something that i love most about my  career is it's constantly evolving and  constantly changing  um at first i i would say  that it's very different than it is now  even i i wanted to be an economist  before i was an actor i wanted to you  know  work in financial planning or  developmental economics or something of  that nature and that's actually what i  went to school for  and you know growing up in kansas city  they have a huge theater community there  as well  and my plan was to work in a financial  institution  and then that closes at five so i can  make it to the theater by eight o'clock  for curtin and it works out perfectly  and that's  that was what i thought my life would be  and i thought how great i can you know  do both things that i love  then i ended up booking a broadway show  and moved to new york and then i went oh  my gosh this is what i'm gonna do for  the rest of my life i'm gonna live in  new york and work in a broadway theater  and this will be fantastic  then i booked a job in la and ten years  later i'm still here  going back and forth doing all these  things so at this point  i'm just open to whatever the future  holds and as long as i get to keep  working in different mediums i'd love to  go back to theater i'd love to do more  film i'd love to do another tv show i i  want to direct  soon i'm developing projects i'm doing  music i'm  kind of putting my hand in everything  because i i think diversity  in creative exploits are what fuels my  soul the most and keeps me growing and  keeps me challenged and that's  that's what i would like to do i think  just um  continuing forward motion is my idea of  success  when you found out that you would be  filming a scene for this where you  essentially have to strangle yourself  what what what goes through your head  what's that like to shoot what's it like  to watch that later  uh watching it's a bit funny i'm i'm a  bit funny about watching myself but  you know that's just part of part of the  job i guess um  i did turn to luke and go is this  because clary killed jonathan in  shadowhunters is this why  this is happening um but you know he  gives the sly luke bain smirk so you  never really know why  but uh but no it was a lot of fun i knew  again  given that luke and i are such good  friends  he knows i have stunt mats at my house  and so he knows that if anyone's  equipped during a pandemic to safely do  stunts  it's me so hence the fact that i end up  doing a lot of the stunts in the film  um but it again it's a lot of fun to get  to  try your hand literally at something new  and  being a huge fan of thrillers and horror  and all of those things growing up  it's fun to get to experiment with what  do i have in my house that can make this  as creepy as possible and how can i  manipulate the few tools that i have  to create an environment um that would  be a real set  how hard is it to figure out those  emotional levels or what you want to do  physically when you don't  have somebody walking you through all  that with you yeah well we were really  lucky i mean nick is such a fantastic  director and is so collaborative  and really honest and that's all you can  ask for from a director is someone who  can kind of guide you  having the scope of the whole film to  sort of help you find those levels and  and  find where it fits in the greater realm  of the story  um but also we were lucky enough to be  able to call on  some folks that we'd worked with before  from shadowhunters in  you know the all the different  departments including stunts  we uh we reached out to a few of our our  stunt  performers and coordinators and friends  from that show who then sent us a video  of hey if we were to do these things in  our house this is how to safely do it  but also make it  look realistic enough on camera and how  to shoot it and where to position the  camera and how to position yourself with  the camera which are normally  all things the stunt coordinator would  tell you and have planned out with the  director  and the camera operators on the day um  but in this case we had  to do it all ourselves so we were really  lucky to have those resources and  and you know given the pandemic a lot of  people had the time  to take a few minutes to to teach us and  we're happy to help given that we all  needed a bit of creativity in our lives  at that point  well i was very impressed with  everything that you pulled off in this  because it it just seemed like there  were endless  possible challenges along the way with  something like this  absolutely and there were but that was  the joy of it is that you know  as creatives and as artists we we always  tend to be overcoming obstacles in one  way or another and this was just  another opportunity to do that so thank  you i'm really glad you enjoyed it  yeah it was very creative so thanks a  lot thank you
 

